# `update auto on`: the machine asks the AI host by itself, fetches a kernel
# that is offered, and restarts to try it. Nobody types anything: this is
# how a new build reaches a machine nobody sits at.
@bootdisk
@net
@ai-net
$ net dhcp
~ 10\.0\.2\.15
$ ai connect {hive} {hive-name} {hive-key}
connected (fake fake)
$ update auto on
automatic updates on (saved)
this machine now fetches kernels the AI host offers and restarts by itself to try them
$ cat /system/update
auto
@offer newer
@restart
~ ^\[slots\] trying slot b \(b\d+\): rollback armed
~ ^\[slots\] slot b passed its health check: it is now the good slot$
$ slots
good slot:   b

# Once the network is up again the host hears that the new kernel is healthy.
$ net dhcp
$ ai
~ beehive online
$ update auto
automatic updates: on
$ update auto off
automatic updates off (saved)
@signal "target": "test", "kind": "health", "text": "slot b b\d+ passed its health check"
